WEBVTT

00:00:00.230 --> 00:00:05.327
Take a complex point z on a circle of radius rho, at angle theta.

00:00:05.607 --> 00:00:09.601
The Joukowski map sends z to omega on the ellipse.

00:00:09.881 --> 00:00:15.589
The pullback expresses P sub n at omega as a function of the original circle angle.

00:00:15.869 --> 00:00:21.964
In the cubic term, five sixteenths is the Laurent coefficient, and three is the exponent.

00:00:22.244 --> 00:00:29.633
At fixed radius, z cubed is rho cubed times the Fourier mode, e to the power three i theta.

00:00:29.913 --> 00:00:37.100
So the Fourier coefficient is five sixteenths times rho cubed, and the mode makes three turns per circuit.

00:00:37.730 --> 00:00:40.319
How can points on a circle trace an ellipse?

00:00:40.599 --> 00:00:43.711
Follow the midpoint between a point and its reciprocal.

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:47.875
Start with a point z on the outer circle.

00:00:48.185 --> 00:00:52.214
Its distance from the origin is rho, which we’ll set to one and a half.

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:56.180
Now mark one over z on the inner circle.

00:00:56.490 --> 00:00:58.933
Join the two points and take their midpoint.

00:00:59.243 --> 00:01:01.809
That’s the Joukowski map, J of z.

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:04.657
Turn z counterclockwise.

00:01:04.967 --> 00:01:08.145
Its reciprocal turns clockwise through the same angle.

00:01:08.455 --> 00:01:09.654
Watch their midpoint.

00:01:09.964 --> 00:01:14.550
As z makes one full turn, the midpoint traces a Bernstein ellipse.

